<edge_devices>
	<!-- One hundred devices, all of them fixed, all of them sending requests: the
		scenario is pure edge, there is no server to fall back on. Twenty five of
		them can compute, the rest are sensors whose requests those twenty five have
		to carry, which is what makes the orchestration decision matter.

		A task runs on a single core, so the speed of a device is the speed of one of
		its cores, and the three computing classes are an order of magnitude apart.
		The fifteen milliseconds of the health monitor are the tightest bound of the
		scenario, and they are tight because of the radio as much as of the processor:
		an offloaded request crosses four wireless hops of one millisecond each, and
		its two hundred million instructions then take two milliseconds on a server,
		eight on a workstation and twenty five on a phone. A phone can thus never
		answer a health monitor that is not its own, and a workstation only when
		nothing else is queued in front of it. That is the trade the orchestrator has
		to learn: the fast devices are the only ones that can carry real time, and
		they are also the ones every background computation would like to have.

		Two devices carry the orchestration. The first is the primary, the second is a
		cold standby that starts switched off and is only brought up when the primary
		crashes, halfway through the run. That is what the fault tolerance of the
		paper is about, and it is why both of them belong to the server class.

		Storage is what makes a fleet that is only a little busy refuse work: a phone
		holds two containers of fifty megabytes at a time, a workstation three, and a
		task that waits in the queue of a device already occupies the storage of its
		container. Five of the twenty five computing devices also start with their
		battery almost flat, below the level at which a device stops accepting work
		that can be done elsewhere, while keeping their own real-time requests, which
		cost them only a few milliseconds. -->

	<device> <!-- the primary orchestrator, a server -->
		<connectivity>wifi</connectivity>
		<mobility>false</mobility>
		<speed>0</speed>
		<minPauseDuration>0</minPauseDuration>
		<maxPauseDuration>0</maxPauseDuration>
		<minMobilityDuration>0</minMobilityDuration>
		<maxMobilityDuration>0</maxMobilityDuration>
		<battery>true</battery>
		<percentage>1</percentage>
		<batteryCapacity>18.75</batteryCapacity>
		<initialBatteryLevel>100</initialBatteryLevel>
		<idleConsumption>0.2</idleConsumption>
		<maxConsumption>5</maxConsumption>
		<isOrchestrator>true</isOrchestrator>
		<generateTasks>true</generateTasks>
		<cores>1</cores>
		<mips>110000</mips>
